[Music]  if every spoon of rice you eat from a  styrofoam box  is toxic to your health and takes days  away from your lifespan would you still  eat from it  should we fix this problem through  design  i think as designers it's really  important to always be  on top of sustainability and have it as  one of our key values  i would say in the last decade there's  been a shift in thinking  from linear product system to more  circular  design system and it's this notion that  you know we should not be  just looking at designing a product  prototyping it  selling it end of story but rather we  have to really look at the whole  ecosystem in a more holistic manner  right from where was the material  sourced from is it farmed or produced in  a sustainable way  right to how we produce it how we sell  it and after the consumer use it  where does it get discarded how do the  cities manage the waste in that manner  and who should be responsible for this  circular system  [Music]  as we all know pulau samaka which is a  landfill for all the  toxic waste that's come from the  incineration is gonna fill up in 2035  so there is urgency right now to reduce  waste  i recall we were at a point in our  professional practice when we wanted to  recalibrate our focus  we were thinking about what we really  care about and how we can make our  design practice  align closer to the purpose that drives  us on a daily basis and i think we're  thinking a lot about nature  so we chose the representation of nature  that  for us is important in singapore which  is a forest and the same in argentina  which is the whales  because we went on a whale-watching trip  there's no real divide between working  together professionally and living a  life together  as a family we come from completely  different cultures with different  traditions and that makes our daily life  more interesting  and it also brings a lot of value to our  professional practice  design is a lens through which you can  see the world and  is a catalyst for change many times  design is done by  to be an engineer would be a factory  owner any layman  can produce design if you look around  you you have a lot of objects that  we're using on a daily basis from the  plastic bag that holds your kopio when  you take away drinks  to the kopitiam cups or the kopitang  chair that we are also familiar with  but we may not know who designed it we  call this anonymous design  design that is really for good  functionality and a good user experience  container  so at the very beginning of every  project we we always start off with  some human centric design methods and  it's critical for us to  understand what are the needs of the  user to even reframe the design brief to  understand  where the opportunities are where the  problem statement is  for this particular project where we are  redesigning food packaging to reduce  single-use plastic  we went into the food centres we were  looking at gestures  behaviors around the way we eat how we  take away we're also looking at the  needs of not just the consumers but also  the food vendors the hawkers how do they  actually pack the food  and how can we design containers that  would better fit their actions and their  gestures so that's easier for them  because if they buy into  that shift in their behavior then they  can also become advocates for  more sustainable systems and the  research that we conduct goes beyond  just  individual users but we also look at the  entire ecosystem there's definitely some  of the gestures that we were studying  that will make a big difference too yeah  even to understanding the  economics of it because at the end of  the day for any of these solutions to be  implemented they have to make financial  sense and  if you come up with a very nice shape  that is very expensive and you cannot  scale it up then it's just not going to  work  so as part of the research is human  centered then  understanding the business or the  finances behind and with all that then  we  kind of have a few brainstorming  sessions where we have all the data that  we collected we have images  to remind us of some specific gesture or  moments in the research from there we  will start sketching come up with some  ideas  quickly prototype them so we can bring  them back to  the user and i guess the reason for the  prototypes is for user testing  especially in this case  when we prototype the the food packaging  we can bring it to the food vendors we  can  use it ourselves and basically keep  testing and seeing how that works  it's the same when we design furniture  we will have the chair prototype in our  place or our studio  for months and anyone who enter would  sit on it and give us some feedback so  to kind of have the time  to let that prototype be used be tested  manufacturing part of design is really  important  the craftsman that is doing what you  design is is a key player and you have  to work with them  adjust the design to have the perfect  use of their  technology then one thing we have to  improve is the  the closing system now is is a bit weak  so we have to make something that is a  bit easier to close  we always visit factories and when we do  chairs we visit carpentries  and that really informs us and helps us  save time  as we develop new projects  [Music]  so designers are storytellers and when  we finish designing a product  we have to start thinking about how to  tell the story and how to tell it  creatively to the wider audience  we do it through you know photography  film  and sometimes even publications so we do  spend quite a bit of time writing  because  sometimes the publication can last  longer than the product itself  and some of the messages and stories or  philosophy can go a long way  in advocating change or in communicating  an idea  and and and seeing how that kind of  build a legacy for the product so from a  designer point of view  our responsibilities in ensuring that  whichever product we design  is easy to disassemble whether it is for  repair or for recycling collaborate with  material scientists to  see what are the better alternatives  that we can use in our case we are  working with  a range of companies on edible materials  we are exploring options with  algae-based materials we are working  with a company  to bring edible container that is made  of wheat so this  initial part of the design process which  is getting the right material or  trying to innovate in new materials is  really important to have a good outcome  yeah i think designers play a very  important role in designing for circular  systems because we understand  how materials can be developed when we  work with material scientists  how factories work how productions work  we can understand what are the user  needs or how can we design for user  needs and we also deal with the  communication  to the users when it comes to the  products that we design so we have that  good overview and that's why we can play  the role very well as facilitators of  the various stakeholders within this  circular system  and i think this is a road that's very  important for for designers to play in  the future  our planet is not going to improve  unless we take action  if you could advocate change through  design would you  [Music]  you

